All seven expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic", "Prediction markets price Lamont's win odds at 96-97 percent", "Incumbent advantage in a Democratic-leaning state"], "rationale": "Every public poll since June shows Lamont ahead, with Quinnipiac and University of New Hampshire surveys both at 55-38 and only one pollster below a 10-point margin. Rating organizations and betting markets align on a comfortable incumbent win, so the evidence supports a mid-teens expected margin with little reason to move.", "watch": "A standout Fazio debate performance or a late shift on cost-of-living and energy-bill issues could narrow a race that has been stable all fall.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Connecticut's governor's race pairs incumbent Democrat Ned Lamont against Republican Ryan Fazio. Lamont enters the final weeks with a clear advantage: recent polls show him ahead by 7 to 17 points, with most surveys in the mid-teens. Quinnipiac and University of New Hampshire polls both put the race at 55-38, and Lamont has led in every public poll this cycle. Rating organizations across the spectrum call the race solidly or safely Democratic, and betting markets give Lamont roughly 96-97 percent odds.\n\nFazio has campaigned on cost of living, energy prices, and data-center tax breaks, and the two have met in debates and dueling fact checks over electric bills and healthcare plans. The state's Democratic lean, Lamont's incumbency, and his early general-election advertising have kept the race from narrowing. A late debate moment or shift on affordability could tighten the margin, but the evidence points to a comfortable Lamont re-election.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.987